    "finish_elapsed_s": 8.593813346000388,
    "text": "A refrigerator works by moving heat from inside to outside, like carrying warmth away in a bucket. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. First, it evaporates inside the fridge, soaking up heat from your food and air. That turns it into a gas. Then a compressor squeezes the gas, making it hot. Next, the hot gas flows through coils behind or under the fridge, where it releases that heat into your kitchen. Finally, it cools down and turns back into liquid, ready to repeat the cycle. So the fridge doesn't \"make cold\"—it removes heat, keeping your food chilly while warming the room slightly."
